          "currency": "USD",
          "description": "Market Research: Foreign Consumer Brands in Korea \n\nWe're a Seoul-based venture that helps international consumer brands grow in the Korean market through talent-led marketing, with a large Korean agency partner. We need a researcher to build a ranked list of foreign (non-Korean) brands that already sell in Korea but are under-marketed i.e. they have distribution, but little visible brand-marketing and no Korean celebrity face.\n\nWHAT WE NEED\nA ranked list of 30-40 foreign-owned consumer brands that:\n1. Already sell in Korea: retail, online, department store, duty-free, or own stores.\n2. Are under-marketed: little visible Korean brand-marketing and no current Korean celebrity/talent endorser.\n3. Sit in a category where a celebrity face moves sales. Priority: beauty/skincare, food & beverage, fashion/apparel, accessories, lifestyle/home, premium. Adjacent consumer/image-driven categories are fine if clearly under-marketed. Skip B2B, industrial, commodity, and regulated (pharma/finance) goods.\n\nRANKING\nRank highest: under-marketed + no current Korean face + a reachable decision-maker. Still log brands that already use a Korean celebrity, but flag them \"has face\" and rank them lower, they're valuable as competitor intel, not primary targets.\n\nCAPTURE PER BRAND (Google Sheet)\n- Brand name\n- Category\n- HQ country / parent company\n- Distribution mode in Korea (importer/distributor, own\n Korean subsidiary, flagship store, department-store counters, duty-free, or online via Coupang/Naver/Olive Young)\n- Korea operator: the importer/distributor or local subsidiary running things on the ground (name it)\n- Year they entered Korea\n- Who runs Korea marketing: title, plus name + LinkedIn if found\n- Current Korean celebrity/talent endorser: who, or \"none found\" \n- Current Korean ad/creative agency, if any: the firm running their Korean ADVERTISING (a creative/network shop). NOT the talent agency that represents a celebrity. Many under-marketed brands won't have one; \"none\" is a positive signal.\n- Recent Korea marketing activity (last ~12 months)\n- Brand's Korean website + Instagram\n- Reachability signals: decision-maker LinkedIn, importer contact, etc.\n- 2+ source links (see Sources)\n- Fit notes: 2-3 sentences: why this is (or isn't) a strong target, how reachable the decision-maker is, and any red flags. Example: \"Strong; 6 yrs via importer, zero celebrity activity while two rivals use K-faces; importer's marketing lead is on LinkedIn. Red flag: HQ may control marketing centrally.\"\n\nSOURCES & ACCURACY (important)\nKorean + English: brand's Korea site/Instagram, Naver news, importer registries, Olive Young/Coupang/department-store listings, LinkedIn, trade press. Mark anything you can't verify as \"not found\". Accuracy beats completeness, and fabricated data or dead links end the engagement. Korean-reading ability is a strong plus.\n\nYOU\nMarket, lead, or B2B prospect-research experience; strong sourcing and accuracy. You cite sources and never fabricate; Korea knowledge + Korean & English strongly preferred.",
